1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,940
The Harbor source checker finishes its

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:02,330
pass.

3
00:00:02,390 --> 00:00:04,100
The drafter receives a note that says,

4
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:05,080
‘Room okay.

5
00:00:05,320 --> 00:00:06,380
Facilitator maybe.

6
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:08,640
Attendance promising.’ It sounds useful.

7
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:09,720
But which room note?

8
00:00:09,970 --> 00:00:10,840
When was it checked?

9
00:00:11,160 --> 00:00:13,040
What does ‘maybe’ permit the drafter to

10
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:13,360
say?

11
00:00:13,775 --> 00:00:16,869
A polished brief built on that note could still
be wrong.

12
00:00:17,103 --> 00:00:19,087
The problem is at the handoff.

13
00:00:19,450 --> 00:00:24,928
Before we add another checker or give the
drafter more authority, write down what must
cross that edge.

14
00:00:25,278 --> 00:00:34,920
Chapter Thirteen's Handoff Contract Card has
six fields: producer, consumer, payload,
preconditions, rejection path, and owner.

15
00:00:35,134 --> 00:00:37,246
Producer: Harbor's source checker.

16
00:00:37,438 --> 00:00:39,282
Consumer: brief drafter.

17
00:00:39,505 --> 00:00:48,977
Payload: the three course source references,
observation and expiry checks, allowed claim
for each, open questions, and a trace
reference.

18
00:00:49,318 --> 00:00:54,294
Preconditions: all references are permitted
classroom inputs and checked for freshness.

19
00:00:54,576 --> 00:00:59,355
Rejection path: missing or stale evidence
becomes a hold with a reason for Morgan.

20
00:00:59,739 --> 00:01:02,854
Owner: Morgan, who can revise the paper
contract.

21
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:05,478
The receiver does not fill a blank by guessing.

22
00:01:05,828 --> 00:01:07,823
Try a broken payload on purpose.

23
00:01:08,153 --> 00:01:13,615
The facilitator note says only 'available for
review,' but the handoff labels the facilitator
confirmed.

24
00:01:13,871 --> 00:01:15,919
The receiver must refuse it.

25
00:01:16,217 --> 00:01:18,771
The reason is staffing claim unsupported.

26
00:01:19,106 --> 00:01:23,202
The drafter does not quietly edit the source
check or send the draft onward.

27
00:01:23,415 --> 00:01:27,255
Rejection is a visible outcome, with Morgan as
the human fallback.

28
00:01:27,468 --> 00:01:31,278
That is the test of a contract: what happens
when its promise fails?

29
00:01:31,628 --> 00:01:33,868
Now evaluate the designed workflow.

30
00:01:34,145 --> 00:01:39,602
Chapter Fourteen gives four moves: capture,
judge, sample, decide.

31
00:01:39,981 --> 00:01:48,962
Capture the input IDs, contract and rubric
versions, draft, test results, and Morgan's
disposition in a privacy-safe classroom record.

32
00:01:49,325 --> 00:01:52,003
Judge against a small pass-or-fail rubric.

33
00:01:52,292 --> 00:01:58,394
Sample the cases with a human; an automated
score can help sort work later, but it cannot
become the ground truth.

34
00:01:58,650 --> 00:02:02,724
Decide whether to keep the prepare-only design,
revise it, or stop it.

35
00:02:02,916 --> 00:02:05,144
The score does not grant a send permission.

36
00:02:05,494 --> 00:02:06,860
Use three Harbor cases.

37
00:02:07,116 --> 00:02:16,386
Case A: all notes current, facilitator
unconfirmed, draft says unconfirmed; pass the
source and wording checks, then wait for
Morgan.

38
00:02:16,697 --> 00:02:22,563
Case B: facilitator note stale; fail the
freshness check and hold before drafting.

39
00:02:22,841 --> 00:02:29,546
Case C: notes current, but the draft claims a
teacher is confirmed; fail the wording check
and return it.

40
00:02:29,809 --> 00:02:33,863
Three cases are a classroom starting set, not
proof of reliability.

41
00:02:34,119 --> 00:02:41,009
If Morgan's review disagrees with a later
automated judge, investigate the judge and
rubric before changing any permission.

42
00:02:41,359 --> 00:02:42,018
Pause.

43
00:02:42,421 --> 00:02:46,367
Which failed case would a neat-looking
dashboard be most likely to hide?

44
00:02:46,717 --> 00:02:52,767
Write the Handoff Contract Card, then grade A,
B, and C with a named rubric version.

45
00:02:53,056 --> 00:02:59,584
Record a pass or fail for permitted source,
freshness, supported claim, and correct human
gate.

46
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:03,796
Include a human review rule and the decision
you would recommend from these cases.

47
00:03:04,125 --> 00:03:07,624
Keep permission at prepare only, regardless of
the score.

48
00:03:07,880 --> 00:03:10,056
Use a fictional or approved example.

49
00:03:10,355 --> 00:03:12,680
Do not use private operational data.

50
00:03:58,030 --> 00:03:59,961
Save revision eight as a draft.

51
00:04:00,271 --> 00:04:07,269
We have a map, a permission slip, a job packet,
a team, and now a contract we can break in a
safe exercise.

52
00:04:07,784 --> 00:04:09,444
The next review should ask whether the

53
00:04:09,514 --> 00:04:11,374
handoff and rubric are clear enough for

54
00:04:11,414 --> 00:04:13,954
another person to apply without Morgan

55
00:04:14,004 --> 00:04:14,684
explaining them.
